          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which neurotransmitter deficiency in the prefrontal cortex best explains the cor</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which neurotransmitter deficiency in the prefrontal cortex best explains the core executive function deficits seen in ADHD?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dopamine and norepinephrine</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Reduced dopamine and norepinephrine activity in the prefrontal cortex impairs inhibitory control, working memory, and sustained attention — the three core executive function deficits in ADHD.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serotonin and GABA</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Serotonin is primarily implicated in mood regulation, and GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter. Neither is the core pathway disrupted in ADHD.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acetylcholine and glutamate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acetylcholine is important for memory circuits and glutamate for excitatory transmission, but these are not the primary neurotransmitters implicated in ADHD.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dopamine and serotonin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dopamine is correct, but serotonin is not the second key neurotransmitter in ADHD. Norepinephrine works alongside dopamine in the prefrontal circuits affected in ADHD.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The two key neurotransmitters in ADHD are dopamine and norepinephrine. Their deficiency in the prefrontal cortex causes impaired inhibitory control, working memory, and sustained attention."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. This neurotransmitter model also explains how stimulant medications like methylphenidate work — by blocking reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine to restore prefrontal function."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
